# Chercan Crawl4AI Project Rules

## License Requirements
- Project uses Crawl4AI v0.5.0 under Apache 2.0 license with attribution clause
- All distributions must clearly attribute Crawl4AI
- See LICENSE file for full legal text and requirements

## Development Philosophy
- Start with specific, working implementations
- Build abstractions from proven patterns
- Document everything, especially successful approaches
- Test thoroughly before generalizing

## Documentation Structure
Following Crawl4AI's organization:
- Setup & Installation
  - Basic setup instructions
  - Environment configuration
  - Post-installation verification
- Quick Start
  - First crawl implementation
  - Markdown generation examples
  - Basic extraction strategies
- Core Components
  - Single-page crawling
  - Browser/crawler parameters
  - Content filtering and caching
- Advanced Features
  - Link & media handling
  - Lazy loading
  - Authentication & proxies
  - Session management
- Extraction Strategies
  - No-LLM approaches (CSS, XPath)
  - LLM-based strategies
  - Chunking and clustering
- API Reference
  - Class documentation
  - Method specifications
  - Configuration options

## Project Structure
- Begin with focused implementations in `/examples`
- Gradually move common patterns to core modules
- Keep working examples as reference implementations
- Use @tasks.md for tracking progress
- @project_specs.md contains detailed requirements and objectives

## Code Standards
- Use Python type hints everywhere
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines
- All classes should have docstrings with parameters and return values
- Create unit tests for all components

## Development Guidelines
- Start with concrete implementations
- Document successful patterns and approaches
- Extract reusable components as patterns emerge
- Handle errors gracefully with custom exception classes

## Crawl4AI Integration
- Import Crawl4AI v0.5.0 and utilize its core components:
  - AsyncWebCrawler for high-performance crawling
  - BrowserConfig and CrawlerRunConfig for configuration
  - Multiple crawler strategies (browser-based and HTTP-only)
  - Deep crawling with configurable strategies (BFS, DFS, Best-First)
  - LLMConfig system for unified LLM provider configuration
  - LLMContentFilter for high-quality markdown generation
  - DefaultMarkdownGenerator with content filters
  - Support for both open-source and closed-source LLM providers

## Initial Architecture
- /examples - Specific implementations
  - /mslearn - MS Learn course extraction
  - /patterns - Successful extraction patterns
- /crawl4ai - Core package (evolving)
  - /core - Base classes and utilities
  - /config - Configuration handling
  - /crawlers - Different crawler implementations
  - /strategies - Crawling strategies
  - /processing - Data extraction and filtering
  - /exports - Export formats
  - /api - API endpoints (future)
  - /cli - Command-line interface
  - /utils - Shared utilities

## Installation Process
- Install full package: `pip install "crawl4ai[all]"`
- Use Python virtual environment for development
- Run post-installation: `crawl4ai-setup` and `crawl4ai-doctor`
- Verify installation with basic test script

## Configuration Standards
- Use environment variables for sensitive data
- Configure browser settings via BrowserConfig
- Set up CrawlerRunConfig for each run
- Use LLMConfig for model configuration
- Implement content filtering where needed

## Testing & Validation
- Test each specific implementation thoroughly
- Validate with real-world data
- Document performance characteristics
- Create example implementations as needed
- Start with MS Learn course extraction example

## LLM Integration
- Support both open-source (ollama/llama3.3) and closed-source (OpenAI) models
- Use appropriate temperature and token settings
- Implement proper error handling for API calls
- Cache results when appropriate
- Follow provider-specific best practices 